            <title>[LU-21043] sanity test_51d: OST object imbalance with more than 4 MDTs 'OST 1 has fewer #0 objects vs. OST 0 (50 &lt; 257 x 4/6)'</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21043</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="error"&gt;&amp;#91;Claude&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt; sanity test_51d fails reproducibly with more than 4 MDTs: the first (stripecount=3) pass finds a large, repeatable OST object imbalance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="preformattedContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;sanity test_51d: @@@@@@ FAIL: stripecount=3:  OST 1 has fewer #0 objects vs. OST 0  (50 &amp;lt; 257 x 4/6)
sanity test_51d: @@@@@@ FAIL: stripecount=3:  OST 1 has fewer objects vs. OST 0  (169 &amp;lt; 375 x 4/5)
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Seen 28 times between 2024-09 and 2026-03 in the many-MDT custom sessions of &lt;a href="https://review.whamcloud.com/56384" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"&gt;https://review.whamcloud.com/56384&lt;/a&gt; (&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-14058" title="Create tests for large number of MDTs" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-14058"&gt;LU-14058&lt;/a&gt; sanity.sh MDT names), with 6, 12, 16, 24, 40 and 48 MDTs and 8 OSTs: about 28 of ~50 such runs. The same numbers recur from run to run. On review-dne-part-1 (4 MDTs) the failure rate is ~0.1% (&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-19093" title="sanity: test 51d stripecount=3: OST 2 has more #0 objects vs. OST 1" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-19093"&gt;LU-19093&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Object counts from the 12-MDT run (test directory is a striped dir -i3 -c2 -H crush2, 800 files):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="preformattedContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;OST0 has 376 objects, 257 are index 0
OST1 has 375 objects, 50 are index 0
OST2 has 376 objects, 52 are index 0
OST3 has 170 objects, 52 are index 0
OST4 has 376 objects, 257 are index 0
...
OST7 has 169 objects, 52 are index 0
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two OSTs four apart get most of the first stripes, and the OSTs before them get about 45% fewer objects, so this is a deterministic round-robin pattern rather than precreate starvation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The part of test_51d that depends on MDSCOUNT is the precreate trigger, which creates MDSCOUNT*2 "setstripe -c -1 wide.$i" files in the test directory before the measured pass. With 12 MDTs that is 24 full-width files on the two dir-stripe MDTs, each of which advances that MDT's round-robin state (lqr_offset_idx) before the measurement, and the wide files are also counted in the per-OST totals (824 first objects for 800 files).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Possible fixes: create the wide precreate files in a separate directory or use a fixed number of them, and/or the &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-8933" title="sanity test_51d: object distribution between OSTs not even" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-8933"&gt;LU-8933&lt;/a&gt; round-robin balance changes (&lt;a href="https://review.whamcloud.com/69305" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"&gt;https://review.whamcloud.com/69305&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;sanity test_51d is currently excluded by 56384 with MDSCOUNT &amp;gt; 4 (under &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-9" title="Optimize weighted QOS Round-Robin allocator" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-9"&gt;LU-9&lt;/a&gt;) and by &lt;a href="https://review.whamcloud.com/69271" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"&gt;https://review.whamcloud.com/69271&lt;/a&gt; with OSTCOUNT &amp;gt; 12 (under &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-8933" title="sanity test_51d: object distribution between OSTs not even" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-8933"&gt;LU-8933&lt;/a&gt;); the 56384 exception should reference this ticket instead.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
